### Page 1 of 3

```text
MEMO

TO:

Hon. Mayor Kaysen and Members of the Cheyenne City Council

FROM:
Jim Flesher, AICP, Senior Planner

SUBJECT:
A Resolution authorizing the Mayor and City Clerk of the City of Cheyenne to
enter into an Amended and Restated Development Agreement with Granite Peak
Development, LP, Clear Creek Land Company, LLC, and Swan Ranch, LLC to
define the rights and obligations of the parties relating to the annexation and
future economic development of the of land generally described as the “Swan
Ranch Development Area.”

DATE:
August 7, 2013

The attached resolution authorizes the Mayor and City Clerk to enter into an Amended and
Restated Development Agreement on the City’s behalf with the owner/developer of the Swan
Ranch Rail Park, in order to clarify provisions of the Original Development Agreement between
the parties, which was recorded May 27, 2011, and to address certain issues that were unforeseen
at the time of the original agreement. Please refer to the attached resolution, Amended and
Restated Development Agreement, Original Development Agreement, and Exhibits for more
information.
The developer has proposed paying the City a fee of $1,000 per acre for each acre sold
(excluding those already sold and several lots under contract) in order to establish a fund for
future improvements in Swan Ranch. In exchange for the fee, the developer would be absolved
of any responsibility to make improvements such as widening roads, should traffic from
development warrant widening.
Site developments necessitating road improvements would not be exempted from having to make
improvements, however. For example, if a large traffic generator, such as a grocery store, were
proposed in Swan Ranch, traffic studies would still be required with the site plan submittal and
the grocery store might need to install acceleration and deceleration lanes and a traffic signal, for
instance, if warranted by the traffic study.

A COMMUNITY OF CHOICE

PLANNING AND DEVELOPMENT SERVICES
URBAN PLANNING OFFICE
2101 O’Neil Avenue, Suite 309, Cheyenne, Wyoming 82001
307.637.6286 jflesher@cheyennecity.org 307.637.6366 (Fax)
```

### Page 2 of 3

```text
Rough estimates of the potential income from the proposed fee upon sale of all property covered
by the development agreement are in the range of $2.5 million (approximately 2,500 acres times
$1,000). Since we don’t know whether roads will require widening in the future it is difficult to
assess whether this is adequate. A rough estimate of the cost of widening the roads with potential
for widening (the “worst case scenario”) is around $6.2 million.
Please note that the development agreement as presented does not exempt future development
from any additional impact fees that may be established to be collected on building permits.
Whoever obtains the building permit for a lot is responsible for payment of the future impact
fees, if adopted. Granite Peak would only have to pay such fees if they construct additional
buildings themselves, rather than selling the land to builders. However, language that was
initially in the proposed agreement regarding measuring traffic to establish a threshold at which
the developer would be required to widen roads has been removed and replaced with the
language now proposed. This removes the uncertainty of future costs for road improvements for
the developer.
While the fee is not intended to replace any future street impact fee on development in Swan
Ranch, it will further the discussion on such fees if we compare the proposed $1,000 per acre fee
with established impact fees in nearby communities. However, attempting to compare the fee to
those collected in Greeley, Fort Collins, and Weld and Larimer Counties is also somewhat
difficult because their fees are based on building size. We can use the Longhaul building (the
building under construction within the annexation area) as an example although the proposed
$1,000/acre fee would not apply to it since it has already sold. The lot is 30.26 acres and the
building is roughly 186,000 square feet. So, had this fee been in place when the lot sold, the City
would have collected $30,260. This would be equivalent to a fee of $162.69 per 1,000 square
feet of building based on the size of the building that was constructed. For comparison, an
industrial building this size would require between $260,400 for the City of Fort Collins and
$494,388 for Larimer County, with Greeley and Weld County falling in between. Please refer to
the table below for more information.
